Here are the main problems in the snippet you gave, plus concrete suggestions (and a revised example) showing how to fix them.

1.  Sequential fetches make it very slow  
    • Your loop does `await fetch` one URL at a time. If you have 20 URLs, each fetch waits for the previous one to finish.  
    • Instead, kick off all your fetches in parallel (or in controlled batches).  

2.  No error‐handling or status checking  
    • You do `await res.json()` without ever checking `res.ok` or catching network/time-out failures.  
    • If one URL 404s or JSON is invalid you’ll get an unhandled exception and the entire call will reject.  

3.  No timeouts or cancellation  
    • `fetch` by itself never times out—if a server hangs, your function hangs too.  
    • Use an `AbortController` (or a library that wraps fetch) to enforce a maximum wait time.  

4.  No input validation / SSRF risk  
    • If you’re passing in user-provided URLs, you can be tricked into fetching internal services (SSRF).  
    • Whitelist domains or at least run new URL(url).protocol checks to ensure only `http:`/`https:`.  

5.  Unbounded concurrency / memory blow-up  
    • If `urls` is huge (thousands of entries), parallelizing them all at once may exhaust sockets or RAM.  
    • Either batch them in chunks or use a concurrency limiter (e.g. [p-limit][1]).  

—  
Below is an example that addresses all of the above:

```js
/**
 * Fetch all URLs in parallel, with:
 *  - Input validation
 *  - Per-request timeout
 *  - HTTP status checking
 *  - Controlled concurrency
 *  - Per-request error handling
 */
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const MAX_CONCURRENT = 5;
const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;

// Fetch with timeout via AbortController
function fetchWithTimeout(url, timeoutMs) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer     = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);

  return fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
    .finally(() => clearTimeout(timer));
}

// Validates that the URL is http or https
function validateUrl(raw) {
  let parsed;
  try {
    parsed = new URL(raw);
  } catch {
    throw new Error(`Invalid URL syntax: ${raw}`);
  }
  if (!['http:', 'https:'].includes(parsed.protocol)) {
    throw new Error(`Unsupported protocol: ${parsed.protocol}`);
  }
  // Optionally enforce a domain whitelist here
  return parsed.toString();
}

async function fetchAll(urls) {
  const limit = pLimit(MAX_CONCURRENT);

  // Map each URL to a limited, validated, timed-out, error-checked fetch
  const tasks = urls.map(rawUrl => limit(async () => {
    const url = validateUrl(rawUrl);

    let res;
    try {
      res = await fetchWithTimeout(url, REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Network/timeout error fetching ${url}:`, err);
      return { url, data: null, error: err.message };
    }

    if (!res.ok) {
      const errMsg = `HTTP ${res.status} (${res.statusText})`;
      console.error(`Bad status for ${url}:`, errMsg);
      return { url, data: null, error: errMsg };
    }

    try {
      const data = await res.json();
      return { url, data, error: null };
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Invalid JSON for ${url}:`, err);
      return { url, data: null, error: 'Invalid JSON' };
    }
  }));

  // Use Promise.all so we wait for all the limited tasks to finish
  const results = await Promise.all(tasks);
  return results;
}

// Usage example
(async function(){
  const urls = [
    'https://api.example.com/1',
    'https://api.example.com/2',
    // ...
  ];

  const results = await fetchAll(urls);
  results.forEach(r => {
    if (r.error) {
      console.warn(`Failed ${r.url}: ${r.error}`);
    } else {
      console.log(`Success ${r.url}:`, r.data);
    }
  });
})();
```

What changed and why:

• We call all fetches in parallel (but limit to `MAX_CONCURRENT` at a time).  
• Each URL is run through `new URL(...)` to prevent SSRF or protocol injection.  
• We wrap each fetch in a 5 second timeout using `AbortController`.  
• We check `res.ok` before calling `.json()`.  
• All network, status and JSON errors are caught locally so one bad URL doesn’t kill the whole batch.  
• We return an object with `{ url, data, error }` so the caller can see exactly which fetches failed vs. succeeded.
